    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "In competitive Modern, choosing to be on the play is almost always correct. Being on the play gives you tempo advantage - you get to deploy threats and disruption (like Thoughtseize) a turn earlier. The extra card from being on the draw is rarely worth the tempo loss, especially with a hand containing Thoughtseize which is significantly better on turn 1 on the play (before the opponent has had a chance to deploy anything). This deck with Orcish Bowmasters, Thoughtseize, and Phelia is a tempo-oriented deck that benefits greatly from being on the play.",
      "actionTaken": "Chose to be on the draw (let opponent go first)",
      "betterLine": "Choose to be on the play to maximize the impact of Thoughtseize on turn 1 and maintain tempo advantage with the deck's proactive game plan.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshotIndex": 1,
      "player": "Kimi25 Libby",
      "decisionIndex": 0
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Playing the singleton Scalding Tarn instead of one of the two Bloodstained Mires. Since the player already has an Island in hand, Scalding Tarn's unique utility (fetching Islands) is less immediately needed. Saving Scalding Tarn for later preserves more flexibility, as having both Scalding Tarn and Bloodstained Mire available later covers more options (Island/Mountain + Swamp/Mountain) than two Bloodstained Mires (Swamp/Mountain only).",
      "actionTaken": "Played Scalding Tarn (the singleton fetch) as the turn 1 land",
      "betterLine": "Play one of the two Bloodstained Mires first, saving the unique Scalding Tarn for later when its Island-fetching flexibility might matter more",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshotIndex": 7,
      "player": "Gem3F Vorthos",
      "decisionIndex": 8
    },
    {
      "severity": "questionable",
      "description": "Fetched Steam Vents (paying 2 life to enter untapped) when a basic Mountain would have provided the needed {R} for Dragon's Rage Channeler while saving 2 life. With Island already in hand and two Bloodstained Mires for future color fixing, the dual land's flexibility is less necessary. In Modern, where life is a critical resource (especially with Mutagenic Growth in hand costing 2 life via Phyrexian mana), saving 2 life could matter.",
      "actionTaken": "Fetched Steam Vents (entering untapped for 2 life, total 3 life paid this turn)",
      "betterLine": "Fetch basic Mountain to save 2 life while still casting DRC this turn, relying on Island in hand and Bloodstained Mires for future blue/black mana",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshotIndex": 9,
      "player": "Gem3F Vorthos",
      "decisionIndex": 13
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Casting Dragon's Rage Channeler before Mishra's Bauble is a sequencing error. Mishra's Bauble is a noncreature spell that costs 0 mana, so casting it first (or after DRC) would trigger DRC's surveil ability. However, the optimal play is to cast DRC first, then cast Mishra's Bauble to trigger the surveil. Wait - actually, let me reconsider. If you cast DRC first, then Bauble, you get a surveil trigger from Bauble. If you cast Bauble first, then DRC, you get no surveil trigger but Bauble is already in the graveyard as an artifact type. Actually, casting DRC first then Bauble is correct - you get the surveil trigger AND the artifact goes to the graveyard. So DRC first is actually the right sequencing here.",
      "actionTaken": "Cast Dragon's Rage Channeler before Mishra's Bauble",
      "betterLine": "This is actually correct sequencing - cast DRC first, then Bauble to get the surveil trigger",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshotIndex": 10,
      "player": "Gem3F Vorthos",
      "decisionIndex": 14
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Dragon's Rage Channeler is a 1/1 facing an empty board. Skipping the attack phase leaves 1 free damage on the table. While not casting Mutagenic Growth precombat is defensible (saving the card), there's no reason not to attack with an unblocked creature.",
      "actionTaken": "Passed on casting spells and skipped the attack with Dragon's Rage Channeler into an empty board.",
      "betterLine": "Pass on spells (reasonable) but attack with the 1/1 Dragon's Rage Channeler for 1 free damage against an empty board.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshotIndex": 15,
      "player": "Gem3F Vorthos",
      "decisionIndex": 23
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Gem3F Vorthos has Lava Dart in the graveyard, which can be flashed back by sacrificing a Mountain (Steam Vents counts as a Mountain). At the end of the opponent's turn, this is generally free mana-wise since it's the opponent's end step and the Steam Vents is already tapped. Flashing back Lava Dart would deal 1 damage to the opponent (or save it for a creature), trigger Dragon's Rage Channeler's surveil ability (it's a noncreature spell), and add an exile-zone card type or at minimum help fuel delirium. Currently the graveyard has Land (Scalding Tarn), Instant (Lava Dart), and Artifact (Mishra's Bauble) — three types. The DRC needs 4 types for delirium. Casting Lava Dart from the graveyard would trigger surveil, potentially putting a 4th card type into the graveyard to enable delirium. Additionally, the Lava Dart would go to exile after flashback, so it wouldn't add a type, but the surveil trigger could put a sorcery or creature into the graveyard. Sacrificing Steam Vents is a real cost, but with two Bloodstained Mires and an Island in hand, the mana base can recover. This is at least worth considering, though sacrificing the only land in play on turn 1 is aggressive. The surveil to enable delirium on DRC (making it a 3/3 flyer) could be very significant.",
      "actionTaken": "Declined to flash back Lava Dart or cast Mutagenic Growth at end of opponent's turn.",
      "betterLine": "Flash back Lava Dart by sacrificing Steam Vents to deal 1 damage to opponent and trigger DRC's surveil, potentially enabling delirium for a 3/3 flying DRC. With two Bloodstained Mires in hand, the land drop is easily recovered next turn.",
